        {
          "subsection_id": "periosteal_elevator_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled dissection: Helps separate tissue along the bone surface.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improved exposure: Provides access to bone for the intended procedure.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Tissue-flap preservation: Supports elevation of periosteal and mucoperiosteal flaps when performed correctly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Versatility: Different sizes and shapes accommodate different anatomical sites.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Relatively simple design: Generally easy to handle and maintain.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reduced need for sharp dissection: Blunt working ends can separate tissue without relying entirely on cutting.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "periosteal_elevator_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive force may tear the periosteum or associated soft tissues.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A blunt or poorly shaped tip may make tissue separation difficult.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A sharp edge can cause unintended tissue injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Limited access in narrow or deep anatomical spaces may make manipulation difficult.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive stripping of the periosteum may compromise local blood supply.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It cannot replace a scalpel or scissors when controlled incision or cutting is required.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "periosteal_elevator_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Periosteal tear",
                      "Excessive force or inappropriate technique may tear the tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Soft-tissue injury",
                      "Surrounding muscles, mucosa, or other structures may be damaged"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bleeding",
                      "Injury to vessels within the periosteum or adjacent tissues may occur"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Haematoma",
                      "Blood may collect within the dissected tissue plane"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Injury to adjacent structures",
                      "Nerves, vessels, cartilage, or other structures may be injured depending on the operative site"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Impaired bone healing",
                      "Excessive periosteal stripping can compromise the blood supply important for bone healing"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue necrosis",
                      "Extensive disruption of local vascular supply may contribute to tissue injury"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "Possible if sterile technique and instrument reprocessing are inadequate"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_12",
              "group_title": "Prevention of complications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Choose the correct instrument for the anatomical site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use controlled movements and appropriate force.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain awareness of nearby nerves, vessels, and other vulnerable structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid unnecessary stripping of the periosteum.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Preserve tissue attachments where they are important for blood supply and healing.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain haemostasis and follow appropriate aseptic technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "periosteal_elevator_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There are few absolute contraindications to using a periosteal elevator, but the technique and extent of dissection must be adapted to the clinical situation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Important precautions include:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid excessive force on fragile or diseased tissue.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not strip more periosteum than required for the procedure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid blind advancement in areas containing major neurovascular structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use an appropriately sized and shaped instrument for confined spaces.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Take particular care when elevating tissue near cartilage, nerves, or thin bone.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Consider the effects of periosteal disruption on bone vascularity and healing.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "periosteal_elevator_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Care, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "For reusable instruments:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Remove gross contamination promptly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Clean the working ends, shaft, handle, and any textured areas.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use a cleaning method compatible with the instrument manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rinse and dry thoroughly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the blade or tip for deformation, burrs, cracks, or damage.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check the handle and shaft for integrity.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterilize using a validated method appropriate for the instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A damaged or deformed working tip should be removed from clinical use until assessed and repaired or replaced as appropriate."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "periosteal_elevator_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "periosteal_elevator_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is a periosteal elevator?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical instrument used to separate and elevate the periosteum and associated soft tissues from the underlying bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the periosteum?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A vascular connective-tissue membrane covering most external bone surfaces, except at articular surfaces covered by cartilage and certain specialized regions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is its main principle of operation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mechanical separation of tissue from bone using controlled pressure and leverage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Mention three types of periosteal elevator."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Molt, Key, and Cobb elevators. The Freer elevator is another commonly encountered related instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is a Molt elevator used for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used to raise mucoperiosteal flaps in oral and maxillofacial surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What is a Cobb elevator used for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is used to elevate paraspinal muscles and associated tissues from the vertebral elements during spinal exposure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why should excessive periosteal stripping be avoided?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The periosteum contributes to the blood supply and healing potential of bone. Excessive stripping can compromise vascularity and impair healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What are the complications of using a periosteal elevator?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Periosteal tears, bleeding, haematoma, injury to adjacent structures, and impaired bone healing from excessive stripping."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. How does a periosteal elevator differ from a bone rongeur?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A periosteal elevator separates soft tissue from bone, whereas a bone rongeur bites and removes small pieces of bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. How does it differ from a curette?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A periosteal elevator separates tissue from a bone surface, whereas a curette has a scoop- or loop-shaped working end used to scrape or remove tissue from a surface or cavity."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
